---
/**
 * `/guides/employer-not-deducting` — from `docs/research/06-apit.md` and
 * `docs/personas/p2-employee-no-apit.md`.
 *
 * UNUSUAL AMONG THESE FIVE PAGES, AND DELIBERATELY SO
 *
 * Research 06 is a research brief, not findings — its own status line reads
 * "unverified — research brief, not findings" and it holds no sources at
 * all. Persona p2's document says outright: "This persona's core claim is
 * unverified. If Q27 comes back contradicted, the persona needs rewriting
 * rather than adjusting." A guidance page for this topic therefore cannot
 * open with the same confidence the other four can — it has to say, in the
 * reader's terms, that the central question is open, while still being
 * useful about the part that *is* settled: the instalment mechanics in
 * research 10 (verified), which apply whatever the answer to the open
 * question turns out to be.
 */
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import AsAtStamp from '../../components/AsAtStamp.astro';
import { Callout, CitationRef } from '../../components/ui/index.ts';
import { loadTaxYears } from '../../lib/tax/load.ts';
import '../../styles/guides.css';

const taxYear = loadTaxYears()[0]!.data;
---

<Base
  title="My employer isn't deducting tax from my pay — am I still liable? — Sri Lanka Tax Return Support"
  description="What is confirmed and what is not yet confirmed about an employee's position when no tax is deducted at source, including the instalment obligation that runs regardless."
>
  <div class="guide measure">
    <p class="guide__back"><a href="/guides/">All guides</a></p>

    <h1 class="guide__question">
      My employer isn't deducting tax from my pay — am I still liable?
    </h1>

    <p class="guide__answer">
      This project has not found and confirmed the specific provision that answers that
      directly, so it is not going to tell you as settled fact. What is confirmed: if your
      employer is not withholding tax on your pay, you are personally an "instalment
      payer" and are expected to pay tax on that income yourself, through the year, by
      four instalments — not once at filing time.{' '}
      <CitationRef src="ira-2017#s.90(1)(b)" sources={taxYear.sources} />{' '}
      That obligation exists whatever the answer to the deeper question turns out to be, so
      it is where this page starts.
    </p>

    <AsAtStamp
      yearOfAssessment={taxYear.yearOfAssessment}
      dataReviewedOn={taxYear.lastReviewed}
    />

    <Callout
      tone="caution"
      title="The central question on this page is open"
      headingLevel={2}
    >
      <p>
        Whether non-deduction by your employer leaves the underlying tax liability with
        you, reduces it, or shifts it to your employer instead is not settled by anything
        this project holds — no source here states it outright. The working assumption,
        used elsewhere in this project, is that withholding is a collection mechanism and
        does not itself create or discharge the liability, which sits with whoever earned
        the income. That is a reasonable general position, but it has not been confirmed
        against the specific Sri Lankan provision, and this page will not present it as
        settled until it has been.
      </p>
    </Callout>

    <section class="guide__section">
      <h2>What decides whether deduction should have happened at all</h2>
      <p>
        Whether an employer is <em>required</em> to deduct tax from your pay — as opposed to
        choosing to, or being exempt from it — is itself not settled by anything this project
        holds. Secondary sources disagree on whether deduction is mandatory for the employer
        or elective with the employee's consent, and this project has not confirmed either reading
        against the Act. That distinction matters: if your employer was never required to deduct,
        nothing has gone wrong and you are simply an instalment payer under the ordinary rule
        for that kind of employment{' '}
        <CitationRef src="ira-2017#s.90(1)(b)" sources={taxYear.sources} />; if your
        employer was required to deduct and did not, that is a different situation this
        project cannot yet describe with confidence.
      </p>
      <p>
        Either way, the practical position for you is the same right now: no tax is being
        taken off your pay, and the instalment mechanics below apply to you regardless of
        which reading turns out to be correct.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="guide__section">
      <h2>The instalment obligation, which is confirmed</h2>
      <p>
        Instalments fall due on or before the 15th of{' '}
        <strong>August</strong> and <strong>November</strong> in the year of assessment,{
          ' '
        }
        <strong>February</strong> in the same year, and{' '}
        <strong>May of the following year of assessment</strong> — that fourth date lands outside
        the year it belongs to, which naive planning misses.{' '}
        <CitationRef src="ira-2017#s.90(2)(a)" sources={taxYear.sources} />{' '}
        Each instalment is not simply a quarter of what you owe: it is worked out from your
        own estimate of tax payable for the year, and a later instalment is recalculated from
        what is left after earlier ones.{' '}
        <CitationRef
          src="ira-2017#s.90(3)"
          sources={taxYear.sources}
          label="Formula from"
        />{' '}
        You are expected to file that estimate with the Commissioner-General by the date the
        first instalment falls due.{' '}
        <CitationRef src="ira-2017#s.91(1)" sources={taxYear.sources} />
      </p>
      <p>
        The exact dates for the year you are working out are on the{' '}
        <a href="/rates/">rates page</a>; the calculator shows you which of them, if any,
        have already passed.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="guide__section">
      <h2>Filing your return</h2>
      <p>
        Separately from the instalments, a return of income is due{' '}
        <strong>eight months after the end of the year of assessment</strong> — a rule the calendar
        derives from, not a fixed date that changes.{' '}
        <CitationRef src="ira-2017#s.93(1)" sources={taxYear.sources} />{' '}
        Filing and paying are not the same act with the same deadline; missing the instalment
        dates does not wait for the filing deadline to become a problem.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="guide__section">
      <h2>If you think you are already behind</h2>
      <p>
        Penalties for late filing and interest on late payment almost certainly apply, but
        this project has not yet confirmed the specific rates or how they accrue, so none
        are stated here. If more than the current year is affected, or you are not sure
        how far back the problem goes, this is worth taking to a qualified tax
        practitioner rather than working through alone — several years of instalment and
        filing obligations interacting is beyond what a calculator should tell you on its
        own.
      </p>
      <p>
        None of this is your fault by default. The tax system's default assumption is that
        deduction happens automatically; when it does not, the obligation still exists but
        nobody told you it did.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="guide__section">
      <h2>What this means if this is your situation</h2>
      <p>
        Work out a figure and see the instalment schedule for your own numbers on the{' '}
        <a href="/calculator/">calculator</a>. If you have not been deducted anything,
        enter that as your APIT figure — it is an ordinary answer, not an error state —
        and the result will show what the instalment obligation looks like for you.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="guide__footer">
      <p class="guide__footer-note">
        This is decision support, not tax advice. Verify with the Inland Revenue
        Department or a qualified tax practitioner before you rely on any figure here to
        file or pay.
      </p>
      <ul class="guide__footer-links">
        <li><a href="/calculator/">Work out your figure</a></li>
        <li><a href="/guides/deadlines/">All the dates that apply</a></li>
        <li><a href="/about/sources/">Sources cited on this page</a></li>
      </ul>
    </section>
  </div>
</Base>
